## Known Limitations & Live Testing Notes

This resource is a `_v1` pilot: schema/model types are generated by
`tfplugingen-framework` from SailPoint's per-service `/identity-profiles/v1`
OpenAPI spec (service folder `identity-profiles`), but
Create/Read/Update/Delete are hand-written against the `golang-sdk`'s
`api_beta.IdentityProfilesAPIService` client.

- **`identity` vs. `identity_profile` scope decision**: SailPoint's
  `/identities` API is GET-only (list+get) - identities are onboarded
  exclusively via authoritative-source aggregation and can never be
  created/updated/deleted directly via API, so an `identity` resource is not
  possible; only a read-only data source would ever make sense there
  (deferred as a follow-up, tracked separately as `identities_v1`).
  `/identity-profiles` is the real Terraform-manageable governance object -
  it binds an authoritative source, defines the identity attribute mapping,
  and assigns lifecycle states for the identities it aggregates - and is
  what this resource manages.
- **Scope is intentionally limited to core Identity Profile CRUD.** This
  resource only covers `POST /identity-profiles/v1`, `GET
  /identity-profiles/v1/{id}`, `PATCH /identity-profiles/v1/{id}` (JSON
  Patch), and `DELETE /identity-profiles/v1/{id}`. The following related
  sub-resource/action endpoints are all explicitly **out of scope** and
  unaffected by this resource: lifecycle states (own sub-resource service,
  mirrors `identitynow_governance_group_v1`'s deferred members/connections
  precedent), identity preview generation, export/import, on-demand sync
  (`SyncIdentityProfile` triggers a refresh, not a CRUD operation), the bulk
  delete-many endpoint, and the default-identity-attribute-config template
  fetch. Managing any of these requires a future, separate resource/data
  source.
- **Only `PATCH /identity-profiles/v1/{id}` (JSON Patch) is used for
  updates.** Per the API's own documentation, `id`, `created`, `modified`,
  `identity_count`, and `identity_refresh_required` are immutable and cannot
  be patched after creation. **`authoritative_source` and
  `identity_attribute_config` cannot both be changed in the same
  `terraform apply`** - the live API explicitly disallows modifying both in
  a single PATCH request; this resource detects that case up front and
  returns an error asking you to split the change across two separate
  applies, rather than sending a request guaranteed to fail.
- **`identity_attribute_config` is a raw JSON string (`jsontypes.Normalized`),
  not a fixed schema.** Each `attributeTransforms` entry's
  `transformDefinition.attributes` shape is entirely dependent on the
  sibling `transformDefinition.type` (an `accountAttribute` transform's
  attributes look nothing like a `rule`, `reference`, or `concatenation`
  transform's) - see this repository's `transform_v1` resource and
  `sources_v1`'s `connector_attributes` for the same modeling pattern
  applied to other dynamic/discriminated-union fields.
- **`authoritative_source.id` is required in practice by the live API, even
  though the schema itself only marks the surrounding
  `authoritative_source` object `Required`, not its nested `id`/`name`/`type`
  fields specifically** (all three are `computed_optional` in the generated
  schema). This mirrors the exact same spec-vs-API mismatch documented for
  `identitynow_governance_group_v1`'s `owner` attribute. Always supply a real
  `id` referencing an existing Source.
- **Delete is asynchronous, unlike every other `_v1` pilot resource's
  Delete().** `DELETE /identity-profiles/v1/{id}` returns `202 Accepted`
  with a background bulk-delete task reference, not an immediate
  synchronous `204`. This resource polls that task's status (via the
  generic `api_beta.TaskManagementAPIService.GetTaskStatus` endpoint) with
  bounded retries/backoff before returning, surfacing a task completion
  status of `Error`/`TerminatedWithErrors`/`Cancelled` as an apply-time
  error rather than silently letting Terraform believe the resource is gone
  before the backend job has actually finished. If the task doesn't
  complete within the bounded wait window, `Delete` returns anyway with a
  warning (the task may still be processing in the background) - Terraform
  will still drop the resource from state at that point, matching every
  other `_v1` pilot's "don't block apply forever" convention for
  eventual-consistency waits.
- **`owner`/`authoritative_source`/`identity_exception_report_reference` are
  associated_external_type-mapped directly onto their SDK structs**
  (`IdentityProfileAllOfOwner`/`IdentityProfileAllOfAuthoritativeSource`/
  `IdentityExceptionReportReference`) rather than hand-converted - all three
  are clean, leaf, all-`*string`-field structs.
